    "failure_scenario": "POST /api/lookup with text '今日は東京で大雨が降っていて電車が止まっています。' has len(text.split()) == 1, giving 400 'text needs at least 5 words to search for', while /api/analyze accepts the same text."
  },
  {
    "file": "signals/textnorm.py",
    "line": 78,
    "summary": "Per-character Thai tokens are built after normalize_text has turned Thai vowel/tone combining marks (\\W in Python re) into spaces, so Thai text keys and MinHash shingles run over consonant skeletons, not real characters.",
    "failure_scenario": "normalize_text('วันนี้ฝนตกหนักมากที่กรุงเทพ') becomes 'ว นน ฝนตกหน กมากท กร งเทพ'. Different Thai words with the same consonants give identical tokens and shingles, which inflates neardup matches. Spaces inserted at each mark also count toward min_text_chars."
  },
  {
    "file": "signals/score.py",
    "line": 174,
    "summary": "semantic_min_tokens gates in score.py and detect.py:37 still count normalize_text(...).split(), not the new textnorm.tokens, so CJK/Thai posts get content keys but never qualify for semantic signals.",
    "failure_scenario": "A Japanese post of 40 characters with no spaces is 1 split-token (< semantic_min_tokens=8), so it is excluded from semantic similarity in content_signals and detect, even though build_keys now treats it as 40 tokens. Coordination scoring is inconsistent across scripts."
  },
  {
    "file": "sources/links.py",
    "line": 18,
    "summary": "The new sentence split handles only full-width 。！？; half-width ! or ? with no following space (common in Chinese/Japanese input) does not split, so the whole multi-sentence text becomes one 'sentence' that is cut blindly at 30 characters.",
    "failure_scenario": "claim_phrase(['你好!我们今天在北京看到了很多人在街上抗议政府的新政策']) returns the whole string, starting with the greeting '你好!', instead of the claim sentence, giving a weaker exact-phrase search."
  },
  {
    "file": "sources/links.py",
    "line": 58,
    "summary": "UNSPACED_RE is applied to raw (non-NFKC) text in links.py, but the ranges exclude half-width katakana (U+FF66-FF9F), so half-width Japanese is treated as one space-separated 'word' and always rejected.",
    "failure_scenario": "claim_phrase(['ｱｲｳｴｵｶｷｸｹｺｻｼｽｾｿ']) returns None ('too short'), while tokens(normalize_text(...)) handles the same text as 10+ tokens after NFKC. The two paths disagree."
  },
  {
    "file": "sources/links.py",
    "line": 108,
    "summary": "The unspaced branch keeps the trailing 。/！ in the phrase (only ,;:、， are stripped), and clusters_for_text does a substring ILIKE, so copies whose punctuation differs at that point won't match.",
    "failure_scenario": "The phrase '今日は東京で大雨が降っていて電車が止まっています。' does not ILIKE-match stored cluster posts that read '…止まっています！' or end without 。, so copy-paste clusters are missed in /api/analyze."
  },
  {
    "file": "signals/keys.py",
    "line": 150,
    "summary": "tokens(norm) is computed twice per post (inside _text_ok and again on the next line), and the new tokens() does a regex search per word plus findall, doubling the work on the hot build_keys loop.",
    "failure_scenario": "For every post in each window, the token list is built twice. Compute toks = tokens(norm) once and pass it to _text_ok (or inline the check) to halve the tokenisation cost."
  },
  {
    "file": "sources/links.py",
    "line": 56,
    "summary": "links.py adds its own unspaced-script measuring (_unspaced_chars/_size/_cut_chars on raw text) instead of reusing textnorm's normalisation/tokens, creating a second, inconsistent definition of 'length' for the same text.",
    "failure_scenario": "Mark handling, NFKC (half-width kana) and min thresholds differ between signals keys (tokens on normalised text) and claim_phrase (raw code points), so a text can be a key but have no phrase, or the reverse. Two code paths must be kept in sync for each script fix."
  }
]
```

